---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import F from "../data/facts.json";
import SceneHead from "../components/SceneHead.astro";
---
<Base title="Public sector: amader.cloud" description="Right-size reviews of public digital projects, and reconditioned devices processed to government standards for data sanitisation, safety, security, radio compliance and e-waste rules." ogDescription="Size public digital projects to the real need, and meet it with reused devices processed to government standards.">
<section class="page-head photo">
<SceneHead id="skyline" />
  <div class="wrap">
    <h1>Public money, sized to the job.</h1>
    <p class="lede">Public digital projects are often specified for the load they might one day reach, not the one they have. We review the plan, size it to the real need, and meet as much of it as possible with reconditioned devices, processed to the standards public bodies have to meet.</p>
    <div class="hero-actions"><a class="btn btn-bulb" href="contact.html?topic=government">Request a right-size review</a><a class="btn btn-line" href="#processing">How we process devices</a></div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap stack">
  <div>
    <h2>Why this matters now</h2>
    <ul class="ledger">
      <li><div class="fig">{F.stats.serverUtil.value}<small>typical server utilisation</small></div><div><p>In a study of US data centres, servers ran at {F.stats.serverUtil.value} of their capacity on average, and up to {F.stats.serverUtil.comatose} were "comatose": still powered, but no longer needed. Capacity bought for a peak that never comes is the most common waste in public and private IT alike.</p><cite>{F.stats.serverUtil.source}</cite></div></li>
      <li><div class="fig">{F.stats.ictWhitePaper.value}<small>ICT white paper</small></div><div><p>The ICT Division's white paper on 15 years of public ICT spending flagged major projects, including the four-tier data centre, for questionable cost justification, and called for stronger independent review.</p><cite>{F.stats.ictWhitePaper.source}</cite></div></li>
      <li><div class="fig">{F.stats.ppr.value}<small>procurement rules</small></div><div><p>The Public Procurement Rules 2025 make e‑GP mandatory, formally introduce sustainable public procurement, and add clear provisions on asset disposal. Right-sizing and reuse now have a place in the rulebook.</p><cite>{F.stats.ppr.source}</cite></div></li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div>
    <h2>The right-size review</h2>
    <p class="measure">A short, fixed-fee engagement before money is committed. It works for a new project, a tender being drafted, or a system that's already running and costs too much.</p>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li><svg class="pict" aria-hidden="true"><use href="#p-books"/></svg><h3>Read the plan</h3><p>The project document, tender specification, or the system as it runs today.</p></li>
      <li><svg class="pict" aria-hidden="true"><use href="#p-server"/></svg><h3>Measure the need</h3><p>Real users, transactions, data volumes, uptime and security requirements, measured where we can and estimated where we can't.</p></li>
      <li><svg class="pict" aria-hidden="true"><use href="#p-hand"/></svg><h3>Map to reuse</h3><p>The agency's own retired devices first, then our reconditioned stock, and new equipment only where nothing else will do.</p></li>
      <li><svg class="pict" aria-hidden="true"><use href="#p-coins"/></svg><h3>Compare</h3><p>New, reused and mixed options side by side: cost, power use, expected life, risk and how each option meets the rules.</p></li>
      <li><svg class="pict" aria-hidden="true"><use href="#p-tablet-log"/></svg><h3>Hand over</h3><p>A report and a right-sized specification your team can use directly in e‑GP tender documents.</p></li>
    </ol>
    <p class="small" style="margin-top:20px">Independence: we can be hired only as reviewers and step aside from supplying that project, so the review is never a sales pitch. We agree a fixed fee in advance and take no commission on what you buy afterwards.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="split">
    <div>
      <svg class="pict" aria-hidden="true"><use href="#p-server"/></svg><h2>What often gets right-sized</h2>
      <ul class="check-list">
        <li>Servers sized for a peak that never comes, replaced by smaller mirrored nodes that can grow when the load does</li>
        <li>New desktops for data entry, kiosks or classrooms, replaced by reconditioned laptops and desktops</li>
        <li>New displays for notice boards and queue screens, replaced by reused monitors and TVs</li>
        <li>Low-traffic portals hosted round the clock in a data centre, while restricted data stays in national facilities</li>
        <li>One large purchase up front, replaced by phases that follow measured use</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="small" style="margin-top:12px">These are patterns we look for, not findings about any particular project.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <svg class="pict" aria-hidden="true"><use href="#p-building"/></svg><h2>Who it's for</h2>
      <ul class="check-list">
        <li>Ministries, divisions and directorates</li>
        <li>City corporations, pourashavas and upazila offices</li>
        <li>Union digital centres and other public service points</li>
        <li>Public schools, colleges, madrasas and universities</li>
        <li>Public hospitals and health centres</li>
        <li>Donor-funded projects and NGOs delivering public programmes</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div id="processing">
    <h2>Government-grade device processing</h2>
    <p class="measure">Every device that goes back into public service is processed, tested and documented so that it can pass an audit. The same pipeline applies to an agency's own retired devices that come to us.</p>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table class="chip-table proc-table">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Area</th><th scope="col">What we do</th><th scope="col">Reference</th><th scope="col">Status</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Chain of custody</td><td>Sealed, numbered crates; two-person handover; reconciliation against the agency's asset register by serial number and asset tag</td><td>Agency asset rules</td><td><span class="status yes">Written procedure</span></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Data sanitisation</td><td>Crypto-erase, firmware secure erase or overwrite, depending on the media; verified, with a certificate per device signed by two people</td><td>NIST SP 800‑88 Rev. 2, IEEE 2883</td><td><span class="status yes">Written procedure</span></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Physical destruction</td><td>On-site shredding for media that fail verification or held restricted data, with a destruction record</td><td>NIST SP 800‑88 Rev. 2</td><td><span class="status maybe">Through a partner</span></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Electrical and battery safety</td><td>Insulation and earth tests on mains equipment; battery health checks; swollen cells removed and recycled</td><td>IEC 62368‑1 (equipment safety)</td><td><span class="status yes">Written procedure</span></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Security baseline</td><td>Clean operating system install, firmware updated, hardened configuration, vulnerability scan before release, admin keys handed to the agency</td><td>CIS Benchmarks</td><td><span class="status yes">Written procedure</span></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Radio compliance</td><td>Only previously type-approved hardware; country set to Bangladesh so power and channels stay within limits</td><td>BTRC rules</td><td><span class="status yes">Written procedure</span></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Data protection and residency</td><td>Personal and restricted data stays on devices or infrastructure in Bangladesh; processing agreements with each agency</td><td>Personal Data Protection Act 2026; Cyber Security Act 2026</td><td><span class="status yes">Written procedure</span></td></tr>
          <tr><td>E‑waste</td><td>Registering with the Department of Environment as a repairer and collection centre; anything we can't reuse goes to formal recyclers, with receipts</td><td>Hazardous Waste (E‑waste) Management Rules 2021</td><td><span class="status maybe">Registration in progress</span></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Records and audit</td><td>A device passport for every unit, exportable to the asset register, with certificates and test results kept for audit</td><td>CAG audit requirements</td><td><span class="status yes">Written procedure</span></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Information security management</td><td>Policies, access control and incident handling for our own systems</td><td>ISO/IEC 27001</td><td><span class="status maybe">Working towards certification</span></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Procurement</td><td>Bids through e‑GP; specifications written to support sustainable public procurement</td><td>Public Procurement Rules 2025</td><td><span class="status maybe">Registering on e‑GP</span></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="small" style="margin-top:14px">Our procedures are written now and will be tested on real devices in our bench phase (Jan–Feb 2027), before the pilot. Rows marked in amber are still in progress. Ask us for the written procedure behind any row.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel-dusk cta">
    <div>
      <h2>Have a project, a tender or a running system?</h2>
      <p style="margin:0">Send us the plan. We'll tell you, for a fixed fee agreed up front, what it really needs and how much of it reused devices can cover.</p>
    </div>
    <a class="btn btn-bulb" href="contact.html?topic=government">Request a right-size review</a>
  </div>
</section>
</Base>
